% 49-self-hosting-ir.weft — Stage 8-final: the middle-end IR builder,
% written in C, compiled by kcc itself, and linked back into the compiler.

\chapter{Self-Hosting: The IR Builder in C}
\label{ch:self-hosting-ir}

The IR builder of Chapters~\ref{ch:ir-design}--\ref{ch:ir-builder} is the
compiler's middle-end spine: it walks the typed AST and produces the neutral,
three-address IR that both back ends---the ARM64 code generator and the x86-64
emitter---consume.  It is by a wide margin the largest module in \kcc{}: the
expression lowering alone (pointer scaling, the integer promotions, struct
copies, bit-field packing, the AAPCS64 argument classification) fills thousands
of lines, and every shape it emits is checked against clang by \texttt{difftest}.
Porting it leaves \emph{only} the raw-syscall entry stubs (\texttt{main},
\texttt{syscalls}) in assembly, so with this chapter the compiler that compiles
\kcc{} is, but for its bootstrap floor, written in the language it accepts.

Because the IR determines every downstream byte---the register a value lands in,
the slot it spills to, the instruction word emitted---this port is verified
exactly as the code generator was: \texttt{difftest} byte-compares every object
\kcc{} produces against clang.  A single different virtual-register number or a
reordered \texttt{emit} would change the machine code and fail the comparison.
The C form is dramatically more compact than the assembly it replaces, because
each \texttt{alloc\_instr}/field-store/\texttt{emit} sequence that built one IR
node collapses to a handful of \texttt{IL()} assignments and one
\texttt{kcc\_ir\_emit()} call.

\section{State, Constants, Externs}

Every IR node is reached through byte offsets---\texttt{IL(p,off)} is the
lvalue at \texttt{p+off}, the C spelling of \texttt{ldr}/\texttt{str
[reg,\#off]}---so the C port reads and writes exactly the fields the assembly
did, at the same displacements.  This module is the definition site of the IR
builder's global state (the current function and block, the function vector, the
locals and label maps, the block-scope journal) and of the module-init flags the
code generator later consults.

@l c
@d Selfhost: ir preamble @{
